{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.4", "U10.5", "U11.4", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The GND1 and GND2 pins of both isolated CAN transceivers (U10, U11) are connected to the same GND net.",
      "expected": "GND1 and GND2 should be on separate isolated grounds (e.g., GND and GND_ISO) to maintain galvanic isolation between the logic side and the CAN bus side.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation of both CAN buses is completely defeated, creating a direct ground connection between the logic and bus sides. This can cause ground loops, noise, and potential damage to the transceivers or connected equipment.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["Q1", "U6"],
      "pins": ["Q1.2", "Q1.3", "U6.1", "U6.6"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "USBC_VBUS_IN"],
      "observed": "Q1 (P-MOSFET) has its source (pin 2) on +5V and drain (pin 3) on USBC_VBUS_IN. U6 (LTC4412) has VIN (pin 1) on USBC_VBUS_IN and SENSE (pin 6) on +5V.",
      "expected": "For the LTC4412 ideal-diode circuit, the PFET source should be connected to VIN (USBC_VBUS_IN) and the drain to VOUT (+5V), so that the controller can properly turn off the PFET and block reverse current.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"Q1\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\") (pintype \"passive\")) on net +5V; (node (ref \"Q1\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"D\") (pintype \"passive\")) on net USBC_VBUS_IN; (node (ref \"U6\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"VIN\") (pintype \"power_in\")) on net USBC_VBUS_IN; (node (ref \"U6\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"SENSE\") (pintype \"input\")) on net +5V"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "USB diode doesn't work, I guess need ideal diode to handle more power? LTC4412 controller and p-ch mosfet. need reverse current protection mostly"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The reverse current protection is defeated. When USB power is removed, the PFET may remain on or conduct through its body diode, allowing the +5V rail to backfeed into the USB port, potentially damaging the USB source or causing unintended power flow.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R36 has a value field of '24k', but its MPN 'FRC0805J240TSD' and description '24Ω' indicate a 24 ohm resistor.",
      "expected": "R36 is a bleeder resistor across the PoE output (VOUTP/VOUTN). A 24kΩ value is appropriate; a 24Ω resistor would draw excessive current and dissipate excessive power.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the BOM uses the MPN, a 24Ω resistor will be installed instead of 24kΩ. This would draw a large current from the PoE output, likely damaging the resistor, the PoE source, or causing a short circuit.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain multiple anomalies. The most severe is that the isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have their isolated grounds shorted together, completely defeating the intended galvanic isolation. Additionally, the LTC4412 ideal-diode PFET Q1 is reversed, defeating reverse current protection, and R36 has a value/MPN mismatch that would install a 24Ω resistor instead of 24kΩ."
}

